# Line-ending policy, decided once here rather than guessed at inside each checker.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# Two failures on this host trace to the same root: a byte-level comparison applied to a text file
# across a platform boundary.
#
#   * BootLoops pins its vendored files by sha256 and refuses to compute on a mismatch. Git for
#     Windows converted LF to CRLF on checkout (that repository ships no .gitattributes), so its
#     pins failed on files that were content-identical. Normalising the newlines reproduced the
#     pins exactly: geo/mc.py hashed 732444e7... raw and 20508a7a... normalised, and the pin is
#     20508a7a...
#   * In this project, typed_decisions/experiment.py in the subject repo carries 156 CRLF pairs
#     while its two sibling PROTECTED files carry none, so a raw-byte guard hash made the verdict a
#     function of which machine looked at the file.
#
# Ten of this repository's own tracked text files are currently CRLF. Declaring the policy here
# means no future checkout can silently change the bytes on disk, and no checker has to guess a
# convention. Checkers still normalise before comparing -- this file makes the *cause* go away, and
# the checkers make the *verdict* independent of the cause.
#
# Consequence to be aware of: after this file lands, a fresh checkout writes LF where the working
# tree previously held CRLF, so those ten files will show as modified in `git status` until they are
# renormalised and committed. That is a real content change and belongs to whoever owns the history.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows shells misparse LF-only batch and PowerShell scripts. Keep them CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Never touch bytes we do not own the interpretation of.
*.gif binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.pt binary
*.bin binary
*.safetensors binary
*.npz binary
*.npy binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
